Yes bhai. Let’s make two very simple diagrams. Don’t worry about Base62 yet — these diagrams only show how the unique ID is generated.

1. Redis Counter Approach

Here, Redis is the central counter.

                    USER
                      |
                      | Create Short URL
                      ↓
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │   URL SERVICE   │
              │                 │
              │  Server 1       │
              │  Server 2       │
              │  Server 3       │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       |
                       | "Give me next ID"
                       ↓
                ┌─────────────┐
                │    REDIS    │
                │             │
                │ url_counter │
                │    = 1000   │
                └──────┬──────┘
                       |
             ┌─────────┼─────────┐
             ↓         ↓         ↓
           1001      1002      1003
             |         |         |
             └─────────┼─────────┘
                       ↓
                  BASE62
                       ↓
                ┌────────────┐
                │  aB92x7K   │
                └─────┬──────┘
                      ↓
                  DATABASE
                      |
              ┌───────┴────────┐
              ↓                ↓
          aB92x7K          xY72p9A
          google.com       youtube.com

What happens here?

Three servers ask Redis:

Server 1 → Redis → 1001
Server 2 → Redis → 1002
Server 3 → Redis → 1003

Redis’s INCR operation makes sure they don’t get the same number.

If Redis goes down:

             URL SERVICE
            /     |     \
           ↓      ↓      ↓
          S1     S2     S3
           \      |      /
            \     |     /
             ❌ REDIS ❌

They can’t get the next ID unless you have a Redis HA/failover setup or another fallback mechanism.

⸻

2. Twitter Snowflake Approach

Now there is no central Redis counter.

Each server generates IDs itself.

                         USER
                           |
                           ↓
                    ┌─────────────┐
                    │ LoadBalancer│
                    └──────┬──────┘
                           |
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             ↓             ↓             ↓
        ┌─────────┐   ┌─────────┐   ┌─────────┐
        │Server 1 │   │Server 2 │   │Server 3 │
        │Worker=1 │   │Worker=2 │   │Worker=3 │
        └────┬────┘   └────┬────┘   └────┬────┘
             |             |             |
             ↓             ↓             ↓
         Snowflake     Snowflake     Snowflake
          Generator      Generator      Generator
             |             |             |
             ↓             ↓             ↓
          ID #1          ID #2          ID #3
             |             |             |
             └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                           ↓
                         BASE62
                           ↓
                     Short Code
                           ↓
                       DATABASE

Each server has something like:

Server 1 → Worker ID = 1
Server 2 → Worker ID = 2
Server 3 → Worker ID = 3

And each generated ID contains:

┌─────────────────┬────────────┬──────────┐
│   Timestamp     │ Worker ID  │ Sequence │
└─────────────────┴────────────┴──────────┘

So:

Server 1:
Timestamp + 1 + 001
Timestamp + 1 + 002
Timestamp + 1 + 003
Server 2:
Timestamp + 2 + 001
Timestamp + 2 + 002
Timestamp + 2 + 003

Even if Server 1 and Server 2 generate an ID at the same millisecond, their Worker IDs are different.

Therefore:

Server 1 → unique ID
Server 2 → different unique ID
Server 3 → different unique ID

